<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giotto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1267–<a href="/years/1337">1337</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Giotto di Bondone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giotto di Bondone was a Florentine painter and architect whose naturalistic figures and sense of space broke with the Byzantine style and laid foundations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> painting. His major works include the frescoes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scrovegni-chapel">Scrovegni Chapel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> of around 1305 and the Bardi and Peruzzi chapels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. He worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert the Wise</a> and designed Florence's campanile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giotto.jpg" alt="Giotto" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto,_Santa_Maria_Novella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
